# The Flue - a sweep, a chimney, and something somebody put up it.
# Written for the library. One person, one procedure, and the question of what to do with a thing that was hidden.

title: The Flue
start: start

== start ==
bg hall_corner
music room_tone
The house is on the list because it has four chimneys and one of them has not been
swept since the old man died, which the daughter tells you on the phone, twice.
You have been doing this fourteen years: rods, brush, sheets, the register, and the
same three sentences said in the same order every time you go into somebody's
front room.
-> the_sheets

== the_sheets ==
You put the sheets down before anything else. That is the first sentence. The
sheets go from the hearth to the door and up over the pictures, and people always
watch the pictures.
daughter: Be careful of the -
you: They're covered. That's what the sheets are for.
She stands in the doorway with her arms folded, which is how people stand when they have decided to be in the way politely.
-> the_first

== the_first ==
The first three are straight: front room, back room, the one over the kitchen. Soot
comes down the way it always does, you take the brush up and bring it down, and the
daughter says something about her father every time you come out of a room.
daughter: He did them himself, always. Even when he couldn't get up the ladder.
daughter: I used to hold the rods.
-> the_landing

== the_landing ==
The fourth is the bedroom, and the bedroom is the one that has not been done, and
the flue for it runs up the outside wall behind the cupboard on the landing.
You open the cupboard. It is a cupboard: a hoover, two tins of paint, a box of
Christmas things, and the register of a man who did his own chimneys in biro on the
back of a calendar.
-> the_landing_look

== the_landing_look ==
The calendar is seven years of Wednesdays, and the last entry is in a different
hand, and it is shorter than the others, and it says *done*, once.
* Take the calendar down. -> take_calendar
* Leave it and get on with the bedroom flue. -> the_bedroom
* Ask her about the calendar. -> ask_her

== take_calendar ==
You take it down. Behind it, in the recess where the flue bends, there is a gap in
the brick about the size of a fist, and in the gap there is a tobacco tin with a
rubber band round it.
You have found things up chimneys before. Coins, mostly, and once a set of keys,
and once a letter that you did not read and handed over folded.
-> the_tin

== the_bedroom ==
You do the bedroom flue, which is the one that needed doing, and the soot comes
down black and wet and takes twenty minutes. When you go back out onto the landing
the cupboard door is open the way you left it and the calendar is still hanging on
the nail.
-> the_fourth_done

== ask_her ==
you: Who wrote the last one?
daughter: I did.
you: It's shorter than the others.
daughter: It was the last time. I did it with the rods out of the shed because he
couldn't and he sat on the stairs and told me when to push.
~ asked_her +1
-> the_calendar_answer

== the_calendar_answer ==
She says it the way people say a thing they have said to themselves a lot.
daughter: He had me do it in September and he died in February and I have not
touched it since, and that's why it's on the list, and I know it's daft.
you: It's not daft. It's a chimney.
daughter: It's not, though.
-> the_tin_or_the_room

== the_tin_or_the_room ==
The brush is out of the flue and the sheets are down and the register is filled in
and this is the part of the job where you decide what you are going to be.
* Open it in front of her. -> the_open
* Put it back exactly as it was and finish the job. -> ending_left_it
* Put it in your bag. -> ending_took_it

== the_tin ==
bg closet
It is a tobacco tin and it is heavier than a tobacco tin. The band has perished and
comes off in your fingers and you open it standing in a cupboard on somebody else's
landing with a brush in your other hand.
Inside there is money, folded, in notes that stopped being printed years ago, and a
photograph of a woman on a bicycle, and nothing with anybody's name on it.
-> the_decision

== the_fourth_done ==
You fill the register in at the front door, which is the third sentence, and she
signs it with her own pen because she did not like the look of yours.
daughter: He'd have hated anybody doing the bedroom one.
you: He'd have hated it being black up there more.
She laughs, once, and it is a real one, and then she pays you and you carry the
rods out to the van and that is the job.
-> END

== the_decision ==
Through the wall you can hear the daughter moving around in the kitchen, putting the
kettle on for somebody who is going to drink it alone.
The tin has been in the brick for a while. Whatever it is, somebody put it there and
did not tell anybody, and the only person who could have is seven years dead.
if asked_her >= 1 -> the_open
-> the_quiet_choice

== the_quiet_choice ==
You could put the tin in your bag. Nobody would know: the cupboard holds a hoover, a
can of paint and a box of Christmas things, and the calendar goes back on its nail
over the gap, and the gap is behind the calendar, and the calendar is behind a
cupboard door on a landing nobody uses.
* Take it. It is nobody's now. -> ending_took_it
* Put it back and say nothing to anybody. -> ending_left_it
-> the_open

== the_open ==
You take it into the kitchen with the sheet still folded over your arm, because
carrying it in a bag would be a different thing.
you: I found something up the bedroom flue.
daughter: What sort of something.
you: It was in a tin. It's money, and a photograph. I haven't counted it.
She puts the kettle down and does not pick it up again.
-> the_open_answer

== the_open_answer ==
daughter: Show me.
You put the tin on the table with the lid off and she looks at the notes for a long
time without touching them, and then she turns it round to see the photograph and
makes a noise that is not a word.
daughter: That's not my mother.
you: No.
daughter: That's the woman from the shop. She had that bicycle until I was nineteen.
She sits down at her own kitchen table and you stand there with a brush in your hand
and neither of you says anything for a while, and then she starts laughing, which is
how it goes sometimes.
daughter: He never said a word. Not once, in all of it.
-> ending_told_her

== ending_told_her ==
You finish the register standing up because she has not offered you a chair, and she
signs it and pays you and adds twenty pounds that you say is not necessary and she
says is not for you, and you drive home with the rods in the back.
It is not your money and it was never going to be, and the part you did was carry a
tin into a kitchen instead of out of it, which is not a thing anybody will ever ask
you about.
You do the same three sentences at the next house and you mean them a little more
than you did, which is the whole of what fourteen years has taught you.
-> END

== ending_left_it ==
You put the band back on as best it will go and put the tin back in the recess and
the calendar back on its nail, and you do the bedroom flue, and you fill the register
in at the front door.
daughter: He'd have hated anybody doing the bedroom one.
you: He'd have hated it being black up there more.
She laughs once. You carry the rods out to the van and drive home and never mention
it to anybody, and the tin is still there now, and so is the calendar over it, and
that is a thing you have decided and will go on deciding.
-> END

== ending_took_it ==
It goes in the bag under the sheets. You finish the register standing up, and she
signs it, and you are home by four with the rods in the back and a tobacco tin in the
cupboard under your own stairs.
The notes are old but a bank will take them, and you know that because you asked,
three days later, at a counter in another town, and then did not do it.
It is under your stairs fourteen years later. You have never opened the photograph
again and you have never thrown the tin away, and you are not sure which of those two
is the thing you are doing.
-> END
